<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="c319ea59">&lt;ul class=&quot;noindent&quot;&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The backlog should be highly visible and easily accessible
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The number of items that can be completed by the end of the project or release depends on the overall / team
        velocity, the number of iterations and the number of teams
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        All items in backlog should be ordered to maximize business value delivered
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        All items in the backlog should be estimated to forecast the completion
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Incomplete items could be left in the backlog if the team is disbanded
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The backlog should not be confused with a &quot;requirements document&quot;
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="45de4555"></attribute>
<attribute name="usageGuidance" nodeId="d29c2938"></attribute>
<attribute name="refinedDescription" nodeId="3aab9193">&lt;p&gt;
    The backlog contains items of varying detail and form. Ultimately it is a list of work items such as features,
    functions, requirements, enhancements, experiments and fixes that are currently deemed necessary to deliver the work
    that are referred to as Backlog Items.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    There are varying levels of granularity within a backlog; items at the top of the backlog that the team has committed
    to delivering soon will likely have greater detail than those scheduled farther out. The lower ordered, and typically
    less detailed, items that are further down in the backlog and will be elaborated or decomposed into smaller items as
    they rise to the top.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The backlog evolves as delivery progresses and is influenced by factors such as changes in requirements, user feedback
    and market conditions. The dynamic nature of the backlog allows it to easily reflect changes in requirements, thereby
    maximizing the value being delivered. However, this also means that the backlog is a living artifact requiring
    continuous maintenance, typically done by a single empowered person.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p/&gt;&lt;p&gt;
    The Product Backlog is ordered such that it starts with more detailed items, or those that the team will commit to
    deliver soon, at the top. The lower ordered, and typically less detailed, items are further down in the Product Backlog
    and will be elaborated or decomposed into smaller Backlog Items as they rise to the top of the Product
    Backlog.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    A single Product Backlog may be used by multiple teams working on the same product. It serves as the single source of
    upcoming work for a given product regardless of the number of teams assigned to developing it.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
